\title{Data egress: what gptr sends to model providers}
\description{
gptr sends data to a model provider only when you run a prompt with that provider's model, or
when model code that the permission mode allowed starts a sub-agent with that model. The package
itself collects nothing: there is no telemetry, no usage reporting and no network
access when the package loads. This page lists what a request contains so that you can decide
what may leave your machine.
}
\details{
R tools compute on objects already in memory; a large dataset does not need to be uploaded
in full for a conversational agent to analyze it. Descriptions and tool results can still
contain data values. Native typed decision inputs use the separate encoding described below.
}
\section{What a request contains}{

\itemize{
\item Your prompt and the conversation so far.
\item With summary context, a budgeted description of each attached object (its class,
dimensions, column names and types and a few values); a small object can appear in full.
\item Automatic context: a short listing of the objects in the evaluation environment (names,
classes and sizes); the objects you added, changed or removed and the top-level R
expressions you ran since the previous prompt (with the output of \code{!code} lines in the
\code{\link[=peter]{peter()}} console); the R version, platform and available packages; and the project
instruction files (\code{AGENTS.md}, \code{CLAUDE.md}, \code{.gptr/vignette.Rmd}).
\item Tool results: the printed output of R code the model ran, the parts of files it read, and
images of plots it made. In \code{manual} mode every action that is not known to be read-only
needs your approval before its result can be sent.
\item With \code{\link[=gptr_coach]{gptr_coach()}}, each coach gets the task, the current coaching and, for each run, its
answer, a summary of its value and objects, the text of the files it wrote and its
transcript, up to 50,000 characters each; the judge gets the task and what each final run
delivered. The coach model can differ from the runs' model.
}

A System 1 question (a classifier model such as Jev) sends the inputs themselves: each
element of a vector (text up to 100,000 characters), each row of a data frame as a record, a
description of larger objects, and for a piped session its last answer (at most
\code{gptr.s1_state_max} characters). Do not ask System 1 questions about data that may not leave
your machine.

Registered secrets, and their URL-encoded, JSON-escaped and base64 forms, are replaced by
markers before anything is sent; a copy the code changes in another way, such as reversed or
cut, is not recognized.
}

\section{Limiting automatic context}{

\code{.opts = list(context = "names")} sends workspace object names, the workspace changes and
attached-object headers, and \code{.opts = list(context = "none")} suppresses the workspace
listing, the workspace changes and attached-object descriptions.
Prompts, tool results and native typed decision inputs have their own paths. Attach only the
objects the task needs. The \code{plan} mode permits actions classified as read-only so you can
inspect a proposed workflow before allowing changes to your workspace.
}

\section{Acknowledgment}{

The first time you use a provider (or a command-line route such as \code{claude_code} or \code{codex})
in an interactive session, gptr shows what is sent and records your
acknowledgment in your user settings. A non-interactive run with a provider that was never
acknowledged stops with a \code{gptr_error_egress} condition. Record the acknowledgment once with
\code{gptr_config(egress = list(anthropic = "ack"), .scope = "user")} (this replaces the stored
list, so name every provider you use). Acknowledgment is required even with
\code{.opts = list(context = "none")}: prompts, tool results, R-session information and project
instructions can still leave the machine. Offline
providers such as \code{\link[=gptr_fake_provider]{gptr_fake_provider()}} and local providers on a loopback address need no
acknowledgment. An Ollama server can forward requests to cloud models, so Ollama is exempt only
while its local-only inference is on (the default; see \code{providers} in \code{\link[=gptr_config]{gptr_config()}}).
}

\section{Other network use}{

gptr contacts nothing else on its own. The model catalog is refreshed only by
\code{gptr_models(refresh = TRUE)}, reachability checks run only with
\code{gptr_providers(check = TRUE)}, and MCP servers, sub-agents and command-line tools are
contacted only when a session uses them. The \code{claude} and \code{codex} command-line routes send
the conversation through those vendors' tools under their own terms.
}

